{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 4,
  "simulation_id": "sim-01",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the magic-wavelength optical lattice clock and its realization as a platform for precision measurement"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B023",
    "B033"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B023 to B014. Fluctuation relations remain a profound theoretical achievement, but the discussion has made clearer that their credit boundary among related relations and contributors is unresolved. B014 instead couples a simple, physically decisive mechanism—magic-wavelength trapping that preserves the clock transition—with a mature, independently reproducible measurement platform. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's decisive experimental development form a focused and defensible allocation, provided that the award is confined to lattice clocks rather than optical metrology as a whole.",
    "case_against": "The proposed two-person credit still requires focused historical review: the distinction between the foundational concept, early realizations, and later performance advances must be established rather than inferred from the maturity of current clocks. The achievement is also an instrument principle, not a general theoretical law; its citation should therefore claim neither all optical-frequency metrology nor every subsequent fundamental application.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks unite the magic-wavelength idea, complementary central contributions by Katori and Ye, independent replication, and durable use in precision measurement.",
        "response": "I find this combination decisive on reassessment. In particular, the reproducibility and demonstrated uses show that the physical principle has become a stable capability rather than a promising technical demonstration. His warning to separate the foundational clock principle from broader optical-frequency metrology should govern the final citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that magic-wavelength confinement permits many neutral atoms to be trapped without perturbing the clock transition, while warning that the broader optical-lattice programme has more diffuse credit.",
        "response": "This identifies the clean mechanism that my initial preference for B023 sought in a theoretical achievement. I agree that the appropriate unit is specifically the magic-wavelength lattice-clock principle and its foundational realization, not neutral-atom research or later clock-comparison advances generally. That narrowness is what makes the two-name configuration presently stronger than alternatives with broader attribution boundaries."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson maintains that circuit QED is an independently mature quantum-optics platform whose merit is distinct from the eventual success of quantum processors.",
        "response": "I accept that distinction and regard B027 as scientifically substantial. Yet the need to delimit foundational circuit QED from a large, continuing platform, together with the question of comparably essential contributors, remains more consequential than for the narrowly stated B014 achievement. This is a judgment about exact credit and present citation scope, not a claim that circuit QED depends solely on commercial or computational outcomes."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival priority review confirms that Katori and Ye alone encompass the decisive conceptual and experimental steps of the magic-wavelength lattice-clock achievement.",
      "Whether the final wording can reliably exclude the distributed later development of optical-frequency metrology while retaining the demonstrated precision-measurement significance of lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the related fluctuation relations can ultimately support a historically complete and sufficiently focused alternative award."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I share the Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but the supplied profile identifies no specific collaboration with them or with any shortlisted candidate. No additional conflict is apparent."
  }
}
